# The Complete Guide to AI Agent Configuration & Extensibility (2025-2026)

Everything you can use to customize, extend, and control AI coding agents — the **files, protocols, and conventions** that form the agent developer's toolkit.

---

## 🧠 The Mental Model

Think of an AI coding agent like a **new team member joining your project**. They need:

| Need | Mechanism | Analogy |
|------|-----------|---------|
| **Who they are** | Rule Files | Job description |
| **How the project works** | AGENTS.md | Project README |
| **Specialized skills** | SKILL.md | Training manuals |
| **Step-by-step playbooks** | Workflows | SOPs / Runbooks |
| **Access to tools** | MCP Servers | Toolbox / API keys |
| **Long-term memory** | Memory Files | Notes / Journal |
| **Live documentation** | Context7 / Resources | Reference library |

---

## 1. 📜 Rule Files — "The Personality & Guardrails"

These files tell the agent **how to behave**, what coding style to follow, and what to avoid. Every major AI tool has its own flavor:

| File / Path | Tool | Format | Scope |
|---|---|---|---|
| `.cursorrules` (legacy) | Cursor | Markdown | Project-level |
| `.cursor/rules/*.md` | Cursor | Markdown (`.md` / `.mdc`) | Project-level, scoped |
| `.windsurfrules` | Windsurf | Plain text | Project-level |
| `global_rules.md` | Windsurf | Markdown | Global |
| `.clinerules/*.md` | Cline | Markdown / Text | Project-level |
| `CLAUDE.md` | Claude Code | Markdown | Project-level memory |
| `GEMINI.md` | Gemini CLI | Markdown | Project-level, hierarchical |
| `.gemini/styleguide.md` | Gemini Code Assist | Markdown | Code review style |
| `.github/copilot-instructions.md` | GitHub Copilot | Markdown | Workspace-level |
| `CONVENTIONS.md` | Aider | Markdown | Coding conventions |
| `.aider.conf.yml` | Aider | YAML | Configuration |

### How They Work

```
                    ┌──────────────────────────┐
                    │   AI Agent starts a task  │
                    └────────────┬─────────────┘
                                 │
                    ┌────────────▼─────────────┐
                    │  Load Global Rules        │  ← User preferences (global_rules.md, ~/.claude.md)
                    ├──────────────────────────┤
                    │  Load Project Rules        │  ← .cursorrules, CLAUDE.md, GEMINI.md
                    ├──────────────────────────┤
                    │  Load Scoped/Local Rules   │  ← Subdirectory-level overrides
                    └────────────┬─────────────┘
                                 │
                    ┌────────────▼─────────────┐
                    │  Merged context applied    │
                    │  to all AI responses       │
                    └──────────────────────────┘
```

### Concrete Example: `.cursorrules`

```markdown
# Project Rules

## Tech Stack
- Framework: Next.js 14 with App Router
- Styling: Tailwind CSS v4
- Database: Supabase (PostgreSQL)
- Language: TypeScript (strict mode)

## Coding Conventions
- Use `const` over `let` wherever possible
- All functions must have JSDoc comments
- Never use `any` type — use `unknown` with type guards
- Prefer server components; use 'use client' only when necessary

## Don'ts
- Never modify files in `/vendor` or `/generated`
- Never commit `.env` files
- Don't use `console.log` — use the project logger
```

> [!TIP]
> **The universal escape hatch**: `AGENTS.md` is becoming the **cross-tool standard** that ALL agents understand (Cursor, Codex, Copilot, Gemini, etc.). If you can only maintain one file, make it `AGENTS.md`.

